 "content": {
  "intro": "",
  "sections": [
   {
    "h2": "Solar system for mosque: sized around prayer times",
    "p": [
     "A mosque's load comes in blocks: fans and lights through each congregation, ACs mainly for Zuhr, Asr and Isha in summer, the water pump for wudu, and a longer, fuller Jummah once a week. The default list averages those hours over the week. It is a Wattlyst estimate for a small one-hall mosque, not a survey of mosques, so change the counts and hours to match yours.",
     "Zuhr and Asr fall in full sun, so the panels carry those prayers directly. Fajr, Maghrib and Isha are in the dark, so their load comes from the grid or a battery. That is why the power rule, 5,540 W ÷ 0.78, decides the panel size here: 7.1 kW against 4.06 kW from the energy rule."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "Solar system for shop",
    "p": [
     "A shop's load follows its opening hours, and most of them are in daylight, which suits solar well. The small-shop list (lights, fans, a deep freezer, a computer, CCTV and Wi-Fi through the opening hours) needs 2.34 kW with a 0.81 kW inverter. With an inverter AC the need rises to 4.68 kW and 2.68 kW.",
     "A freezer or a cold drinks fridge runs day and night. If the shop is closed at night and the grid is off, the freezer needs a battery or it warms up; add backup hours above, but list only the freezer, CCTV and router for that battery."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "Commercial solar calculator for a small building: what changes",
    "p": [
     "The sizing is the same as for a house; only the load list and the hours change. What differs is the bill: shops are on the commercial tariff and some mosques have their own arrangements, while our tariff data covers domestic connections only. This page therefore sizes the system but does not compute the bill saving; take the units a month from the result and your rate from your bill.",
     "A building that is empty at night but busy at midday gets more from solar than a house, because more of its units are used directly instead of exported at the buyback rate."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h2": "What does a solar system for a mosque or shop cost?",
    "p": [
     "We do not print a price on this page because panel and inverter prices move with the dollar rate. The solar price estimator gives the current range for a 7.61 kW system from dated price lists, and the quote checker compares a quote you have against that range.",
     "A complete quote includes the panels, the inverter, mounting structure, DC and AC cables, breakers, surge protection, earthing and installation, and for a grid-tied system the net-billing application. Mosque committees often collect donations against a quote: ask for an itemised quote so donors can see what each part costs."
    ]
   }
  ],
  "faq": [
   {
    "q": "How many kW of solar does a mosque need?",
    "a": "The small one-hall mosque here needs about 7.61 kW with a 6.93 kW inverter; the larger mosque with 2 ton ACs about 16.38 kW. Enter your own fans, lights, ACs and hours for your figure."
   },
   {
    "q": "Can a mosque run its ACs on solar?",
    "a": "In daylight, yes: Zuhr and Asr fall in full sun, and the panels are sized so the running load of 5,540 W, ACs included, runs on sun at midday. Maghrib, Isha and Fajr run from the grid or a battery."
   },
   {
    "q": "What size solar system does a small shop need?",
    "a": "About 2.34 kW with a 0.81 kW inverter for lights, fans, a deep freezer, a computer, CCTV and Wi-Fi through the day, and about 4.68 kW with one inverter AC."
   },
   {
    "q": "Does a mosque need a battery with solar?",
    "a": "Only for power during load shedding after dark. A grid-tied system without a battery is the cheapest and covers the daytime prayers; add backup hours above for Maghrib and Isha, listing fans and lights only."
   },
   {
    "q": "How much does a solar system for a mosque cost?",
    "a": "Prices change with the dollar rate, so we do not print one here. Use the solar price estimator for the current range for 7.61 kW, and ask installers for an itemised quote."
   },
   {
    "q": "Where do the mosque and shop load lists come from?",
    "a": "They are Wattlyst estimates from typical small mosque and shop fit-outs in Pakistan, with appliance watts from our appliance list and summer hours. They are starting points, not measurements; every row can be changed."
   }
  ],
  "key_facts": [
   "A small one-hall mosque in Lahore with fans, lights, 1.5 ton inverter ACs, a water pump, a dispenser and a sound system runs about 5,540 W and uses about 16.2 units a day, so it needs about 7.61 kW of solar and a 6.93 kW inverter.",
   "A small shop open all day with lights, fans, a deep freezer, a computer, CCTV and Wi-Fi uses about 8.4 units a day and needs about 2.34 kW of solar with a 0.81 kW inverter.",
   "Adding a 1.5 ton inverter AC for most of the opening hours to that shop raises the need to about 4.68 kW of solar and a 2.68 kW inverter.",
   "The mosque and shop load lists on this page are Wattlyst estimates from typical fit-outs, not measurements of any one building; change every row to match yours before you ask for quotes."
  ],
  "mistakes": [
   "Sizing a mosque from its yearly bill. The winter bill is small and the summer Zuhr load with ACs is large; the panels must carry the summer running load at midday.",
   "Using the default list without checking. The mosque and shop lists are estimates; count your own fans, lights and ACs and the hours they run.",
   "Forgetting the water pump for wudu. It runs for a short time but its starting surge sets the inverter's surge rating.",
   "Sizing a battery for every load. Night prayers usually need fans and lights only; list those alone for a battery you can afford.",
   "Assuming a domestic tariff for a shop. Shops are billed on the commercial tariff; use the rate on your own bill for the saving."
  ],
  "sources": [
   {
